A||||8||black-86-GT||2.8 modified vs 3.4||||1||
Z||000000||black-86-GT||01-19-2001||08:47 AM||||Hi all Im planning this summer to do a complete overhaul on (Precious) My 87 GT. I was wondering which would be most cost effective with the most power. Rebuilding my 2.8 with all of the goddies like roller cam bored and all that stuff. Or going ahead and try to find a 3.4 and doing the same stuff with that. I know there are quite a bit of mods that have to be done to accomadate the 3.4. So i figured i would get on here and ask for some professional advise. Thanx all this is a great forum.||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||01-19-2001||08:55 AM||||I'd say get ahold of the Fiero Factory in Toney, Al and get his already built ready to drop in 3.5-3.6. it'll save lots of time and aggrivation. and like the old saying goes.. theres no replacement for displacement. you'll be hard pressed to get the 200+lbft of torque out of a 2.8... well naturally aspirated anyway.<P>i don't have his web address.. but i'm sure some one here does.. Moo i think just picked up a car from there last week..||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||01-19-2001||04:07 PM||||did someone say they didnt have Ed's web address?!<P>give this a try....<BR> <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A> <P>and if you would like to email him....<P>fierofactory@juno.com<P>and if youi would like to snail mail him....<P><B>Ed Parks    (The Fiero Factory)</B><BR><B>8710B Hwy 53, Toney, Ala 35773</B><P>and if you would like to call the shop....<P><B>Shop # 256-420-5391</B>    <P>or home....<P><B>Hm # 256-882-1568</B><P>hope this helps!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-19-2001||07:22 PM||||The 3.4-liter engine will cost you allot more but the 3.4-liter engine will make allot more power and torque, especially on the bottom end of the rpm band. If you are going to spend the money than go all the way and get WHAT YOU WANT.||||reg||1
Z||000004||black-86-GT||01-20-2001||11:43 PM||||well what kind of mods would i have to do to the chassis to make the 3.4 fit. im pretty sure i can find a 3.4 in the junk yard some where and build it up. my question is how much more am i looking at spending im kind of on a tight budget. thanx all<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||01-21-2001||01:02 AM||||I spent about $800 more so far. If you are on a tight budget than you may want to just keep the 2.8-liter engine. The block alone was $400. Everything cost more for the 3.4-liter engine compared to the 2.8-liter engine when it comes to rebuilding them. Parts for the 2.8-liter engine are more plentiful.<P>I like torque so I don't care how much it costs and there is no better way to get more torque than with more displacement.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||01-21-2001||03:54 PM||||When I was doing my 2.8, it was before the advent of the 3.4. So I went all out on the 2.8. Stress relieved the block and heads, ported, polished and cc'd the heads, 3 angle valve job, a slightly more agressive cam, match ported everything from the intake to the exhaust, roller tip rockers and a 2.5 inch exhaust. I had decided against boring and stroking because I wanted to keep some semblance of gas mileage and ran out of money before I could get headers. Had I done those final mods I think I would have been able to be almost as powerful as a stock 3.4. <P>With that said, I always have to make sure I have a napkin ready for the drool when I think of doing what I did to the 2.8 to a 3.4...<P>I have a good strong responsive engine and Im very proud of it since I was the one who built it (took me almost a year to finish) but I always wish that I had gone 3.4.||||reg||6
Z||000007||black-86-GT||01-22-2001||01:05 AM||||I think I've made up my mind I think im going to go with the 3.4. Because i am on a tight budget and i still need my car. I cant afford to have it out of commision for to long. Im probably going to see if I cant find one for cheap then build the engine as I go. then when its done ill go ahead and put it in. Because the saying is right. there is no replacment for displacement. ||||reg||14
Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||01-22-2001||09:47 AM||||For the full cost of my 3.2 rebuild, including the shop fees and all, I probrably could have gotten a crate 3.4 and remounted the starter. The 3.2 I built up is about 205 ft/lbs 170 hp. Basically, the 2.8 w/ a 3.1 crank, bored .040 over, and a slightly more aggressive cam. It's a nice set up, I have plenty of power off the line, but you can tell the top end runs out of air. Now if I had a Getrag 5 speed or one of those OD autos I'd be happy.||||reg||1
